  c_name: "Marie Curie", c_zeit: "1867–1934", c_kurz: "Half-life: how fast does a radioactive substance decay?",
  c_story: "Marie Skłodowska Curie grew up in Warsaw and did her research in Paris. With her husband Pierre she discovered the elements polonium and radium and studied radioactivity – the word is hers. She won two Nobel Prizes, in physics (1903) and chemistry (1911), and is still the only person to win in two different sciences. Radioactive substances decay by a fixed rule: in every half-life, half of the remaining atoms decay.",
  c_weiter: "One more half-life", c_reset: "Start again", c_noch: "Half-lives: {n} · {a} of 100 atoms left", c_erwartet: "Rule: 100 · (½)^{n} = {e}",
